<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guillaume Farel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as William Farel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guillaume Farel was a French evangelist and Protestant reformer who spread the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in French-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. He preached at Montbéliard in 1524–1525, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in 1530 and helped win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1535, where he persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> to stay in 1536. He later served as pastor in Neuchâtel until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guillaume-farel.jpg" alt="Guillaume Farel" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William-Farel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Farel was born in 1489 at Gap in the French province of Dauphiné, the son of Antoine Farel, a papal notary, and Anastasie d'Orcières. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1509 and studied with pro-reform Catholic clergy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>. There he met the humanist scholar Jacques Lefèvre d'Étaples, who helped him obtain a professorship of grammar and philosophy at the Collège du Cardinal Lemoine.</p><p>With Lefèvre he joined the Cercle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a>, gathered from 1519 by the reform-minded bishop Guillaume Briçonnet, and by 1522 the bishop had appointed him a diocesan preacher. The circle emphasised the study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and a return to the theology of the early Church. Farel came under the influence of Lutheran ideas there and, after condemnation by the Sorbonne, evangelised in the Dauphiné.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Writings against the use of images in worship forced Farel to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In 1524, in Basel, he wrote thirteen theses sharply criticising Roman doctrine, and the argument was so heated that even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> joined in the demand for his expulsion. He went on to Strasbourg and Montbéliard, then to Zürich to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and back to Strasbourg, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a>. He preached through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, in Aigle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, Orbe, Grandson and Yverdon, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> adhered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1530. At the Synod of Chanforan in 1532 he took part in the discussions that led the Waldensians to merge with the Reformation.</p><p>Farel began preaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1532 under the protection of Bern. After a hostile reception he gained followers, and the General Council adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In 1536 he persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, who was passing through, to stay. In 1538 Farel and Calvin were banished after conflict with the city's government over the authority of Church and State, and Farel settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> as its first pastor for the rest of his life. He returned to Geneva after Calvin did in 1541. In 1558, aged 69, he married the 18-year-old Marie Thorel; Calvin refused to bless the marriage. He died in Neuchâtel on 13 September 1565.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a> named Farel, with Calvin and Pierre Viret, one of the triumvirate of the Francophone Reformation. In 1533 he installed the first fully Reformed printer, Pierre de Vingle, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>. He published around fifteen works, all in French, among them the first French-language work of the Reformation, Le Pater noster et le Credo en françoys of 1524, and the first systematic exposition of Reformed doctrine in French, of 1529. His doctrine was Christocentric, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> he stressed, like Zwingli, that the bread and wine signify the body and blood of the Lord. A monument to him was unveiled at Neuchâtel on 4 May 1876.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Farel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Guillaume Farel</b> preached there in 1524–1525, and under the Württemberg rulers it later became Lutheran, an unusual Protestant enclave between Catholic Franche-Comté and Alsace.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">County of Montbéliard</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1536 Bern conquered Vaud from Savoy, took Lausanne and expelled the bishop, introducing the Reformation after the Lausanne Disputation in which Calvin and <b>Farel</b> spoke.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-lausanne">Prince-Bishopric of Lausanne</a></li><li><blockquote>The county became Protestant under <b>Guillaume Farel</b> in 1530 and was closely allied with Bern and other Swiss cantons.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-neuchatel">Principality of Neuchâtel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gap, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.56,6.08&amp;year=1538" aria-label="Gap on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4695 588 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guillaume Farel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5145 858h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5145 858h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.56,6.08&amp;year=1538">What was here in 1538? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
